.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-webhooks-update - Update a webhook's name, callback URL, or topic


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 webhooks update  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Change a webhook's name, callback URL, or topic in place. The id and the shared
secret are kept, so the receiver keeps verifying with the secret it already has.

.PP
This does NOT change whether the webhook is active. Repointing a broken webhook
at a URL that works leaves it exactly as inactive as it was — which is the
second half of the repair sequence worth knowing:
.IP "  1." 5
\&'webhooks event-callbacks  --status failed'   what was it failing on
.IP "  2." 5
\&'webhooks update  --callback-url …'           fix it
.IP "  3." 5
\&'webhooks reset-circuit \&'                     let it deliver again

.PP
Step 3 is not optional after a tripped breaker, and doing it before step 2 just
spends another 10 events reopening it.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-u\fP, \fB--callback-url\fP=""
	New callback URL

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for update

.PP
\fB-n\fP, \fB--name\fP=""
	New webhook name

.PP
\fB-t\fP, \fB--topic\fP=""
	New topic


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 webhooks update whk_abc123 --callback-url https://example.com/new
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-webhooks(1)\fP
